- op: discuss  # ddd8
  at: '2026-11-17T14:15:31Z'  # ddd8
  by: agent  # ddd8
  actor: jgstern_agent  # ddd8
  clock: 4  # ddd8
  nonce: ddd8  # ddd8
  message: "Merged in PR #2227 (dev eec3215e6). BUILT: `coverage-select graph-rebuild --commit C --hg H [--build B] [--after-pid P]` and, in scripts/smart-test, stop_graph_rebuild (run as soon as a run holds the lock) and start_graph_rebuild (called before pytest on an explicit-path run and after the installed hypergumbo is found on every other). WHAT IT REBUILDS, AND THE THRESHOLD THE RULING LEFT TO ME: the graph of the branch's BASELINE commit (merge-base with origin/dev), whenever no stored graph of that commit, from the installed analyzer build and with no uncommitted files, exists. With that graph a selection has no seed that is not the change's own. CLOSE CONDITION, RUN: (1) 'does not start while the smart-test lock is held': test_it_does_not_start_while_a_test_run_has_the_checkout (it reads smart-test's pid file; it does not take the lock, which would refuse a run starting in that instant); and a starting smart-test stops a running one: test_a_run_that_starts_stops_the_rebuild_and_only_the_rebuild, and LIVE: '[smart-test] stopped a graph rebuild (pid 775535)' with 'stopped after 660 s: a test run has the machine' in rebuild.log. (2) A LIVE DETACHED REBUILD after a real run: 'stored graph 2 of 99ac1c34f (68954 nodes, 275187 edges, build dfca1edd8196f6bb) in 398 s' (60 s of that its settle wait); its checkout and its 350 MB survey were gone afterwards. PEAK MEMORY WAS NOT MEASURED in that run (the plan's figure for the same analysis is 4.2 GB; the rebuild refuses to start under 6,000 MB free). (3) The next `smart-test --manifest` with a source edit: 14 s, 'selecting from stored graph 2 of 99ac1c34f, 0 commit(s) old: 8 seed file(s), 0 of them not this change's, reaching 0 test file(s) this change does not'. rebuild/live.txt. (4) Documented in the smart-test playbook ('The graph that answers exactly, made in the background'). (5) 27 mutations, 26 red; the survivor was `git worktree add --force`, not needed on git 2.43, removed (rebuild/mutations.tsv). DIFFERS FROM THE DESCRIPTION: the description said each rebuild appends its comparison to stale_graph_observations.jsonl. It does not: the rebuild analyses the baseline commit, not the tree a selection was made for, so there is no selection to compare. The comparison still runs whenever a selection itself analyses (stale-record). 'On every --full' is covered by the same start on every run. FOUND: a process that ended and was not collected by its parent still answers a signal, so the wait for the starter could hang; a zombie counts as ended. The rebuild does not prune git's worktree list (it would drop other worktrees whose directory is missing). KNOWN LIMIT: runs that follow one another without about seven idle minutes (an agent's pre-PR chain) stop it each time; the first selection on a new baseline then analyses in the foreground as before."  # ddd8
